/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  PROPERTY SYSTEM OF THE PURIFICATION SECTOR -- and the reason it exists.

  `constant/` lives at EVERY level of a fractal case, because what we KNOW is
  not the same everywhere in a plant.  This sector is the proof.

  The plant's own package (../constant/thermoPhysPropDict) declares AMMONIA as
  the solvent: in the synthesis loop the liquid phase is liquid ammonia with
  dissolved H2, N2 and Ar, and the Henry pairs are fitted for that solvent.
  Correct in LOOP and in PRODUCT.

  In THIS sector there is no ammonia at all.  The only liquid that ever appears
  is the condensate dropping out of the knock-out drum, and it is essentially
  pure WATER -- the water the deoxo made out of the oxygen the industrial
  nitrogen brought in.  Asking an ammonia package to describe it would put the
  dissolved gases on the wrong Henry rung (gases are about ten times LESS
  soluble in water than in ammonia).

  So the sector declares its own world: same components, same SRK vapour, but
  WATER as the solvent and the *-water pairs from the catalogue.

  HOW MUCH DOES IT CHANGE?  Very little -- the drum only carries ~4.5 kmol/h of
  liquid, so the correction moves a few ppm of the feed.  It is here because it
  is RIGHT, not because it is large, and because it is the mechanism the purge
  scrubber will need, where the solvent really is water and the amount really
  does matter.

  THE STREAM THAT LEAVES THIS SECTOR CROSSES A MODEL BOUNDARY.  `Makeup` goes
  from this world into the plant's; (T, P, z) are held and each side recomputes
  its own enthalpy, so H STEPS at the boundary.  The run prints that step in
  the first-law ledger.  It is the two models disagreeing on H(T,P,z) -- it is
  information, not a bug.  H is the conserved truth; T is the model-dependent
  readout.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 2;

components       ( N2  H2  NH3  Ar  water  O2 );

equilibrium
{
    formulation diluteSolution;

    liquid
    {
        solvent
        {
            component     water;
            standardState pureLiquid;          // the Raoult rung
        }
        solutes
        {
            components    ( N2 H2 Ar O2 );
            standardState infiniteDilution;    // the Henry rung
            solutionModel henryDilute;
            binaryParameters
            {
                N2-water { source "data/standards/parameters/Henry/N2-water.dat"; }
                H2-water { source "data/standards/parameters/Henry/H2-water.dat"; }
                Ar-water { source "data/standards/parameters/Henry/Ar-water.dat"; }
                O2-water { source "data/standards/parameters/Henry/O2-water.dat"; }
            }
        }
    }

    vapour
    {
        fugacityModel SRK;
    }
}
